//! What Kumi can change in Renoise (M3): tracks (made, moved, grouped and deleted too), the song's tempo, lines per beat
//! and ticks per line, a track's DSP devices and their parameters, a pattern track's lines, the pattern sequence's slots
//! and the loop; and Lua run in Renoise for the rest (M4). Each kind is the tool's (`payload/changes_*.lua`).
use kumi_daw_own::changes::{OwnAction, OwnChangeSet, OwnKind, Picture};
use kumi_types::contracts::ChangeFamily::*;
use kumi_types::daw::DawProfile;

const PLAN: &str = "Make changes in Renoise in one call, in order: each step is one of your change tools or a step below with its input, and \"@name\" in an input stands for what an earlier step marked as: \"name\" made (a new device). A wait step ({\"beats\": 8} or {\"seconds\": 4}) pauses between steps. Each change is one step of Renoise's undo. It stops at the first step that fails and says what was done. With final: true and every step done, Kumi tells the producer what changed and the answer ends there, with no reply from you: use it when the changes complete the request, even a single change.";
const UNDO: &str = "Undo one of your changes from this session: pass its change id (such as c3), or \"last\" for the latest one. Kumi puts back exactly what that change set, as a new step of Renoise's undo; it only works while nobody changed the same thing in Renoise since, and if so, say what happened (discard: true puts it back anyway, only when the producer asks). With final: true, when the undo is all that was asked, Kumi tells the producer and the answer ends there, with no reply from you.";
const INSTRUCTIONS: &str = "Change Renoise with your change tools, or several changes at once with make_changes, using the refs your reads gave. Each change is one step of Renoise's undo (mute, solo, the loop, the metronome switch and the editing settings aren't: Renoise's undo skips them), and undo_change takes back one of yours as a new step (Renoise's own undo is the producer's). Numbers are Renoise's own, as it shows them: lines, patterns, instruments and samples count from 0 (00 the first; a row's index or number, and a note's instrument, are these), and tracks, phrases, sequence slots and places in a chain from 1; a track's first device is its mixer, so devices go at 2 or later. Adding, deleting or moving a device makes Renoise's refs new: read again before using older ones (a ref the change made comes back with it). Pattern lines are written in Renoise's own notation (note \"C-4\", \"OFF\"; two-digit hex values; effect \"0A20\"). A slot of the pattern sequence is its ref (seq:3); its section, its pattern and its tracks' mutes change by it. The observation's kumiChanges lists your latest changes and where each stands (applied, undone, kept, unsure, or expired: Kumi can't undo it any more, and its note says who can); the producer can undo one in Renoise or in HISTORY without telling you, so go by kumiChanges and Renoise, not by memory. Sounds become an instrument with instrument_kit, one change: a drum kit (a key per sound, its hi-hats choking) or a multi-sample by root notes; write the pattern with the keys its answer lists.";

pub static CHANGES: OwnChangeSet = OwnChangeSet {
    kinds: &KINDS,
    actions: &ACTIONS,
    plan_description: PLAN,
    undo_description: UNDO,
    instructions: INSTRUCTIONS,
    plan_steps: false,
    most_track_adds: Some(32),
};

